---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: A task this host dispatches, in the layout every plan task here carries.
variables:
  what:
    description: >-
      The change, stated concretely enough that the worker can act on it without guessing:
      what to build or alter, where, and what it must not touch.
    type: text
    required: true
  why:
    description: >-
      The impact that motivates the change, in product-owner terms: what it does for the
      product, its users, development and resources, which is what the worker and its
      judge weigh a trade-off against. It never attributes or quotes the request (no "the
      user asked", no "the user said"): who asked for what, in their own words, is
      recorded once, in the `**Requested**` part of the plan description's overview.
    type: text
    required: true
  goals:
    description: >-
      The outcomes this task must reach, one string per goal, or `[]` when it states none.
      A goal is a concrete, checkable outcome that must be true when the work is done. It is
      distinct from the Why, which is the impact that motivates the work, and from the
      acceptance criteria, which are the checks that prove the goals were reached. Goals
      include non-functional ones (speed, latency, CPU, memory, disk, harness quota or
      cost), and each non-functional goal names the `id` of the budget behind it: one a
      task of the plan owns, or an existing root budget of the repository. A goal is proven
      by acceptance criteria, by budgets, or by both, and goals and criteria relate many to
      many, with no pairing assumed: one criterion may prove several goals, and one goal
      may need several criteria and budgets together.
    type: list
    items: string
    default: []
  additional_info:
    description: >-
      The task's own notes and grants, rendered under `## Additional info` above this host's
      operational notes: context the worker needs that is not a criterion, and any carve-out
      the operational notes say only a task may grant. Leave empty when there is none.
    type: text
    default: ""
  spikes:
    description: >-
      The spikes this task builds on, one object per spike with three string keys: `spike`
      (the spike's node id, `spike-<topic>`), `report` (the document id of its report,
      `<plan native id>-<spike id>-report`, a document of this plan's own project) and `branch` (the
      preserved branch its harness is kept on, ending `/<spike id>`, or `/<spike id>-<n>`
      where a retry of the spike cut its own). Rendered as a `## Spike
      evidence` section, so the worker starts from the measurements and the harness. Leave
      empty when the task builds on no spike.
    type: list
    items: object
    default: []
  boundary:
    description: >-
      The engine's mixed-plan context for this node, exactly as the engine states it in a
      mixed plan's `## Repository boundary` section: an object whose `mixed` is true and
      whose `clearance` is `public` or `private`. Answer it only for a node of a plan that
      spans public and private repositories; a public node's task then carries the
      no-private-reference criterion and its appendix the boundary note. Leave it unanswered
      for every other plan, which renders exactly as it would without it.
    type: object
    default: {}
---
{% extends "onepipeline/plan-task.md.j2" %}
{% block before_criteria %}
## What

{{ what }}

## Why

{{ why }}

{% if goals %}
## Goals

{% for goal in goals %}
- {{ goal | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% include "plan-task-budgets.md.j2" %}
{% endblock %}
{% block more_criteria %}
{#-
  DERIVED BUDGET CRITERIA. A dispatch's judge is bound only by the acceptance criteria, so a
  budget stated in `## Budgets` alone binds nobody. Each budget this task adds or changes is
  therefore rendered here as one criterion derived from its record, after the authored ones,
  and no authored criterion restates it. A budget in a project's own budgets file is held to
  onebudgetspec's own verdict, exactly the rule the gate applies. A budget in the
  repository's root `budgets.yaml` is repo-wide and the merge path enforces its threshold, so
  its criterion asks for its registration and its measurement, never a within-target result,
  and for its registration alone where only the complete gate takes its figure, because a
  dispatch never runs that gate. The record says only which file a budget is in, so whether a
  budget is gate-measured or `host-variable` is a condition each criterion states for the
  worker to read off the registered budget. A budget the task leaves as it is gets none.
-#}
{% for budget in budgets if budget.file_change in ["add", "change"] %}
{% if budget.file != "budgets.yaml" %}
- Budget `{{ budget.id }}` is registered in `{{ budget.file }}` as `## Budgets` states it, and its command, run over the finished tree inside this dispatch at the workload `## Budgets` states for it ({{ budget.workload | replace("\n", " ") }}), gets onebudgetspec's own verdict for that budget within its target, by the same rule the gate applies and no stricter or looser. Where onebudgetspec only warns on that budget (one labelled `host-variable`), an overrun does not fail this criterion; the dispatch's report instead states the measured figure, the threshold and the host load the reporter printed.
{% else %}
- Budget `{{ budget.id }}` is registered in `{{ budget.file }}` as `## Budgets` states it, with its command, and that command has run over the finished tree inside this dispatch at the workload `## Budgets` states for it ({{ budget.workload | replace("\n", " ") }}), the dispatch's report stating the figure it measured, the threshold and onebudgetspec's verdict. It is a repo-wide budget whose threshold the merge path enforces, so no within-target result is demanded here. Where its registered command is the repository's complete gate, or its figure is taken only by running that gate, only its registration with its command is required and no figure is demanded, because the merge path runs that gate and this dispatch does not.
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if boundary.mixed is defined and boundary.mixed and boundary.clearance is defined and boundary.clearance == "public" %}
- Nothing this task produces names anything private: no private repository, project, plan, task, path, identifier or content appears in its files, paths, names or identifiers, its commit messages or branch, or the text of its change request, follow-up drafts and reports.
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block after_criteria %}
{% if spikes %}

## Spike evidence

This task builds on these spikes. Start from each report's measurements and from the
harness on each branch rather than measuring again. A report is the document of this plan's
own project with that id or, on a copy of the plan, the one whose `onetaskgraph.origin`
names it:

{% for entry in spikes %}
- `{{ entry.spike }}`: report `{{ entry.report }}`, a document of this plan's own project; branch `{{ entry.branch }}`
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if additional_info %}

## Additional info

{{ additional_info }}
{% endif %}

{% include "dispatch-appendix.md" %}
{% endblock %}
